    "created_at": "2023-02-24T17:38:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-02-24T17:38:03Z",
    "body": "> We could look at the element's video tracks to say whether there should be an image, but they could go away and the element would still show an old image that we should be able to draw.\r\n\r\nIf the page explicitly disables/de-selects a video track, shouldn't a call to `drawImage` with that video element fail? Same question for if the video track \"goes away\".\r\n\r\nIOW, if the intent of `drawImage()` is to render into a canvas exactly what is visible on screen, perhaps a more strict check of the presence of a `selected` `videoTrack` is actually appropriate, since an element without a `selected` `videoTrack` [shouldn't render](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#the-video-element:dom-videotrack-selected). (IIUC)\r\n\r\n> Perhaps it'd be better to look at `videoWidth` and `videoHeight`:\r\n\r\nThose properties are set by the time the element reaches `HAVE_METADATA`, but there's no guarantee a decoded video frame is available for drawing (or displaying). But again, changing the `selected` `videoTrack` should result in a `\"resize\"` event and should modify `videoWidth` and `videoHeight` anyway.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
