    "updated_at": "2023-03-10T22:03:18Z",
    "body": "It's been a while since I filed this, but yes, I agree looking at the presence of a selected video track makes sense. I.e. to return _bad_ if there is no selected video track.\r\n\r\n> if the intent of `drawImage()` is to render into a canvas exactly what is visible on screen\r\n\r\nThat does not seem to be the intent of `drawImage()` because\r\n\r\n> - ↪ [HTMLVideoElement](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#check-the-usability-of-the-image-argument)\r\n>   - If image's [readyState](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#dom-media-readystate) attribute is either [HAVE_NOTHING](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#dom-media-have_nothing) or [HAVE_METADATA](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#dom-media-have_metadata), then return bad.\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n> [3](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#drawing-images:dom-context-2d-drawimage-3). If usability is bad, then return (without drawing anything).\r\n\r\nSo if a video element has previously been drawn to a canvas, then after having called drawImage for the same canvas again but now with a selected video track with no frame for the current time, the canvas will render the previous frame, whereas the video element will render nothing.\r\n\r\nFirefox follows this and draws nothing if there is no current video frame.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
